 69CU    9BE(70ZN,69CUG)               2016KU11                  26NDS    202601
 69CU  H TYP=FUL$AUT=C.D. NESARAJA$CIT=NDS 207, 1 (2026)$CUT=1-Apr-2023$        
 69CU c  2016Ku11: {+69}Cu was produced via the single-proton removal from      
 69CU2c  the primary beam {+70}Zn. At RIKEN, the reaction was studied by        
 69CU2c  impinging the {+70}Zn beam at E=63.13 MeV/nucleon on a 101.46          
 69CU3c  mg/cm{+2} {+9}Be target at the entrance of RIPS spectrometer.          
 69CU4c  Beam identification was done using a Si detector via the               
 69CU5c  |DE -time-of flight technique. The detector was later replaced by      
 69CU6c  a plastic scintillator during the data taking to increase the          
 69CU7c  implantation rate. The plastic scintillator provided start signal      
 69CU8c  for the isomeric lifetime measurement. Four coaxial HPGe detectors were
 69CU9c  used to detect the |g-rays at |q=|+135|? and |q=|+45|? with respect to 
 69CU9c  the beam axis. The {+69}Cu nuclei were implanted on a 1 mm Cu foil     
 69CU9c  positioned between the poles of an electromagnet that provided         
 69CUac  an external magnetic field of 0.50 T {I1}. E|g, I|g, mult, E(particle),
 69CUbc  particle-|g coin. were measured. Deduced T{-1/2} using the time        
 69CUcc  difference between the implantation and correlated |g-transitions,     
 69CUdc  experimental time dependent perturbation function R(t), constructed    
 69CUec  by energy-gated time spectra, spin alignment {I|a}, and g-factor using 
 69CUfc  Time-dependent Perturbed Angular Distribution (TDPAD) method.          
 69CU c  The spin alignment {I|a} =-3.3 % {I9} was measured for {+69}Cu         
 69CU2c  populated in single-nucleon removal reaction using the amplitude of    
 69CU3c  the oscillating R(t) function and assuming E2 character for 189.7|g.   
 69CU cG M$Multipolarities given by 2016Ku11 are based on the observed phase    
 69CU2cG difference of oscillations between the L=2 and L=1 transitions in      
 69CU3cG time dependent perturbation function (R(t))spectra. It should be       
 69CU4cG noted that there is no clear explanation on how the shape of R(t)      
 69CU5cG spectra differ from M1 to E1 (or E2 to M2) transitions in 2016Ku11,    
 69CU6cG therefore the evaluator has assumed that the electric and magnetic     
 69CU7cG character of the transitions were deduced from the J{+|p} values of    
 69CU8cG the excited states adopted from the Adopted Levels of {+69}Cu.         
 69CU9cG The evaluator has included these multipolarities by 2016Ku11 in        
 69CUacG the comments.                                                          
